{"feedback": "The implementation faithfully follows the experiment plan: events are correctly tagged for initial vs final phonemes, epochs are extracted and filtered in the theta band, power is computed via the Hilbert envelope, and session‐wise averages and a paired t‐test (with Cohen’s d) are performed. Quality checks for NaNs are included. 

Results summarize mean theta power per session (initial vs final), t(9) = 1.00, p = 0.343, Cohen’s d = 0.316, indicating no significant difference. The bar plot illustrates these means with error bars. The deliverables (means table, test statistics, effect size, and plot) are provided. 

In conclusion, there is no significant evidence that phoneme position (initial vs final) affects theta‐band power in this dataset.", "success": true}

{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Words composed of more phonemes evoke stronger theta‐band (4–8 Hz) power responses than words with fewer phonemes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether theta power around word onsets scales with word complexity (number of phonemes).",  
      "steps":"1. Load MEG data and events for all sessions. 2. From each session’s events.tsv, select kind=='word' rows and for each word identify its phoneme children via matching sentenceidx and wordidx; count phonemeidx per word to derive a complexity measure. 3. Bin words into 'low complexity' (≤median phoneme count) and 'high complexity' (>median). 4. Epoch MEG data from –0.1 s to +0.4 s around each word onset (timemeg). 5. Bandpass filter epochs in 4–8 Hz, compute Hilbert‐envelope power, and average across 0–0.3 s post‐onset and across channels to yield one theta power value per word. 6. For each session, compute mean theta power for low‐ vs. high‐complexity words. 7. Across sessions, perform a paired t-test comparing high vs. low complexity theta power.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of session-wise mean theta power for low and high complexity words. 2. Paired t-test statistic, p-value, and effect size (Cohen’s d). 3. Boxplot or bar plot comparing theta power by complexity."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer words (in character count) produce deeper beta‐band (13–30 Hz) suppression following word onset than shorter words.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether word length modulates post-onset beta suppression.",  
      "steps":"1. Read events.tsv for each session; select kind=='word' and compute word_length by len(segment). 2. Divide words into quartiles by word_length. 3. For each word, epoch MEG data –0.1 s to +0.5 s around timemeg, bandpass filter in 13–30 Hz, compute Hilbert‐envelope amplitude, and baseline-correct using –0.1 to 0 s window. 4. Compute mean suppression (baseline minus post-onset 0–0.3 s amplitude) per epoch and average across channels. 5. For each length quartile and session, compute mean suppression. 6. Use repeated‐measures ANOVA across quartiles to test for length effect.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Session-wise beta suppression values by length quartile. 2. ANOVA table (F, p) and post-hoc comparisons. 3. Line plot of suppression vs. length quartile."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Delta‐band (1–4 Hz) phase‐locking increases at sentence onsets relative to mid-sentence word onsets.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare delta phase concentration at the moment of sentence onset vs later words.",  
      "steps":"1. Load events; identify sentence boundary events by selecting word events with wordidx==1. 2. For comparison, sample an equal number of mid-sentence word events (e.g., wordidx>1) per session. 3. Epoch MEG data –0.2 s to +0.5 s around timemeg for both sets. 4. Bandpass filter in 1–4 Hz, extract instantaneous phase via Hilbert transform. 5. Compute phase-locking value (PLV) across epochs at time zero for each channel, then average across a set of channels (e.g., all magnetometers). 6. Per session, compare PLV at sentence onset vs mid-sentence using a paired test.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Session-wise PLV at sentence vs mid-sentence. 2. Paired t-test results (t, p). 3. Topographic map of PLV difference."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Baseline alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) power differs systematically between magnetometer and gradiometer channels during silence.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify differences in alpha baseline between sensor types.",  
      "steps":"1. From events.tsv, select all silence segments ≥0.5 s per session. 2. For each segment, epoch MEG data over the segment duration and bandpass filter in 8–12 Hz. 3. Compute power via squared Hilbert envelope and average over time. 4. For each segment, compute median power separately over magnetometer channels and over gradiometers. 5. For each session, aggregate across segments by median, yielding one mag and one grad alpha value. 6. Across sessions, perform a paired t-test (mag vs grad).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of session mag vs grad alpha power. 2. Paired t-test statistic and effect size. 3. Bar plot with sensor‐type comparison."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Phoneme duration positively correlates with the magnitude of post-onset theta‐band power (4–8 Hz).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test correlation between individual phoneme durations and their induced theta power.",  
      "steps":"1. Load phoneme events for all sessions; for each, record duration and timemeg. 2. Epoch –0.1 s to +0.4 s around each phoneme, bandpass filter 4–8 Hz, compute squared Hilbert envelope power averaged over 0–0.3 s post-onset and across channels to obtain one power value per phoneme. 3. For each session, compute Pearson (or Spearman) correlation between duration and theta power. 4. Combine session‐wise correlations via Fisher’s Z and test whether the average differs from zero.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Session correlation coefficients and p-values. 2. Combined Z-score and p-value. 3. Scatter plot with regression for one representative session."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer preceding silence intervals lead to stronger post-word onset beta rebound (13–30 Hz).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine how the length of the silence immediately before a word modulates beta‐band rebound.",  
      "steps":"1. Identify word events that are preceded by a silence event; compute preceding_silence_duration for each word. 2. Bin words by preceding silence into tertiles (short, medium, long). 3. Epoch MEG data –0.2 s to +0.5 s around word timemeg, bandpass filter 13–30 Hz, compute baseline‐corrected envelope amplitude. 4. Quantify beta rebound as peak amplitude within 0.1–0.3 s post-onset. 5. For each tertile and session, average rebound across words and channels. 6. Use repeated‐measures ANOVA to test effect of silence length.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Rebound amplitudes by tertile and session. 2. ANOVA results and planned comparisons. 3. Line or bar chart of rebound vs silence tertile."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Gamma‐band (30–50 Hz) inter‐sensor coherence between left‐temporal and right‐temporal regions is higher during word presentation than during matched silence periods.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare gamma coherence between homotopic regions in word vs silence epochs.",  
      "steps":"1. Define left‐ and right‐temporal sensor groups from channel_names. 2. From events.tsv, sample an equal number of word onsets and silence midpoints (matched in time distribution). 3. For each event, epoch MEG data –0.1 s to +0.4 s and bandpass filter 30–50 Hz. 4. Compute pairwise coherence between all left‐temporal and right‐temporal channel pairs over 0–0.3 s per epoch, then average to yield one coherence value per epoch. 5. For each session and condition (word vs silence), average coherence across epochs. 6. Across sessions, perform a paired t-test comparing conditions.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Session‐wise mean gamma coherence for word and silence. 2. Paired t-test results and effect size. 3. Plot of coherence by condition."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Broadband (1–100 Hz) power measured during very long silence (>2 s) segments drifts systematically across sessions, indicating inter‐session baseline changes.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether baseline noise floor (broadband power in silence) changes across recording sessions.",  
      "steps":"1. From events.tsv of each session, select silence segments ≥2 s. 2. For each segment, extract MEG data over the segment and compute total power by summing squared signal across 1–100 Hz (use wideband filter or FFT integration) and averaging over time and channels. 3. For each session, average across silence segments to yield one broadband baseline value. 4. Plot baseline values across session index and fit a linear trend. 5. Test slope of trend for significance via linear regression.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of session index vs broadband baseline power. 2. Regression slope, intercept, and p-value. 3. Line plot with fitted trendline."  
    }  
  }  
]}
